      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bell Arthur first grew around a Norfolk and Southern Railroad line that arrived in 1907, turning the area into a budding community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://digital.lib.ecu.edu/ncpi/view/23004",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town officially became Arthur in 1911, marking its first legal incorporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://digital.lib.ecu.edu/ncpi/view/23004",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1933, Arthur was renamed Bell‑Arthur, a change that reflected local pride and history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://digital.lib.ecu.edu/ncpi/view/23004",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bell‑Arthur lost its municipal charter in 1966, ending its status as an incorporated town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://digital.lib.ecu.edu/ncpi/view/23004",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1916, Bell‑Arthur gained brief notoriety as the home of “Gar Gar” Edwards, a three‑year‑old who famously smoked cigars.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
